{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>The horse is prepared for the day of battle, but salvation is YHWH's (Proverbs 21:31).</b>" "<b>King Solomon,</b> upon him be peace, admonishes every person with this verse (Proverbs 21) that he should do everything he needs to do, and by way of nature whatever is in his power, and surrender the rest to Heaven, for a miracle does not come into effect except where nature is deficient. And the foundation of man's creation is built upon the attribute of nature; therefore a person needs to perform actions and measures that serve as preparations to attain his desire and to arrive at his heart's requests — like one who wishes to go to war against his enemies: it is fitting for him to prepare weapons and horses and chariots for the day of battle, for if he does not prepare and relies on a miracle, he will be delivered into the hand of his enemies. Or like one who has a sick person: it is fitting for him to prepare poultices and medicines for his food, and to feed him beneficial foods and to withhold harmful foods from him; and after he has done all he can and exerted all his strength and made all his preparations by way of nature, it is not fitting for him to trust that his desire will be achieved except by the Name, may He be exalted, not by these preparations — for there is a man who perishes in battle despite all the preparations, and there is one who is saved without them; and there is a sick person who dies despite the beneficial foods, and there is one who is healed by the bad, harmful foods. If so, the essence of salvation in the matter of war and in the matter of healing lies not in their preparations but in the Name, may He be exalted, as it is written (Psalms 147:3): ״הרופא לשבורי לב ומחבש לעצבותם״, and it is written (ibid.): ״לא בגבורת הסוס יחפץ ולא בשוקי האיש ירצה״, and it is written (Psalms 33): ״שקר הסוס לתשועה״. This is the intent of the Torah, and most of the verses prove likewise. Therefore the Torah commands Israel to exert themselves in these preparations — that they go forth as an armed host when they go up to war against their enemies, and that they set ambushes and send spies into the cities of the enemies — for all these matters are preparations to do what is within human power to do by way of custom and nature, and afterward the miracle operates upon the entire deed of nature. Even though Israel did not need this, because their victory was not by way of custom and nature but only according to merit and punishment — if they were worthy, a few of them would conquer many nations, and if they were guilty, a few of the enemies would conquer many of them — nevertheless the commandment of the Torah comes to Israel to do all their exertion in these preparations and afterward to surrender the matter to the One to whom salvation belongs, for the Torah never relies on a miracle. And for this reason the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded Noah regarding the making of the ark, for it is known that it was within His power, may He be exalted, to pass Noah and his sons over the waters of the flood on foot, and Noah would not need to conduct himself in this by way of nature — to make an ark of a specific wood and a specific measure in length and width. But the Torah commands us in this, that we exert ourselves in preparations in all matters, and with all our exertion our faith shall be that the essence of salvation lies not in them but in the Name, may He be exalted. And this is what Solomon said here: 'The horse is prepared for the day of battle, but salvation is YHWH's.' And so we find with David, upon him be peace, when he inquired through the Urim and Tummim, the Holy One, blessed be He, answered him (II Samuel 5:23): ״הסב אל אחריהם ובאת להם ממול בכאים״ — He commanded him to exert himself by way of custom and nature, like the custom of the other nations who are victorious by nature. And so we find in the battle of Ai, that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joshua (Joshua 8:2): ״שים לך אורב לעיר מאחריה״. And this is the very same reason regarding the spies whom Moses our Teacher, upon him be peace, sent in the wilderness of Paran, that the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded in this. And this is what is written." "<b>Send for yourself men, and let them spy out</b> etc. The verse says: after they requested the matter and desired it, as they said (Deuteronomy 1:22): ״נשלחה אנשים לפנינו ויחפרו לנו את הארץ״ — 'Send men before us and let them search out the land for us' — 'send for yourself men' who know the roads and the approaches to the cities, in the manner of what is written (Judges 1:24): ״הראנו נא את מבוא העיר״, and who will give them counsel as to which city they should do battle with first and from which side it will be convenient for them to conquer the land." "<b>And Rashi,</b> of blessed memory, interpreted 'send for yourself' — 'according to your own discretion; I am not commanding you.' The Master's intent was to say that even though the sending of the spies was by the word of YHWH and by His command, the command was not according to His will, but only in order to fulfill the desire of Israel who wanted this. And it is like the matter of the king: the command of the Name was (Deuteronomy 17:15): ״שום תשים עליך מלך״, but it was not according to His will, for thus the Prophet testifies (Hosea 13:11): ״אתן לך מלך באפי ואקח בעברתי״." "<b>And there are</b> those who interpret 'send for yourself' as 'for your benefit,' in the manner of (Genesis 6:21): ״ואתה קח לך מכל מאכל״, (Numbers 10:2): ״עשה לך שתי חצוצרות כסף״, (Deuteronomy 10:1): ״ועשית לך ארון עץ״, and so every 'for yourself' (לך) in the Torah. And the benefit was that the days of Moses were lengthened all the days of the wilderness, for had their sin not occurred they would have entered the Land immediately, and it would have been impossible for him to enter, even had there not been the decree of the sin of the Rock, for it had already been decreed upon him at the time of his mission to Pharaoh, when the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him (Exodus 6:1): ״עתה תראה״ — and he would not see the war of the thirty-one kings." "<b>And why</b> the spies were twelve, when it would have sufficed for there to be two or three, as it says in Joshua (Joshua 2:1): ״שנים אנשים מרגלים חרש לאמר״ — is because the Holy One, blessed be He, desired that this mission be with the agreement of all the tribes of Israel, one man from each and every tribe, so that if they succeed in their mission all Israel would be partners in the merit, and if they do not succeed all of them would be partners in the punishment, as indeed happened to them." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> the spies whom Moses sent were fools; the spies whom Joshua sent were righteous, as it says (ibid.): ״וישלח יהושע בן נון מן השטים שנים אנשים מרגלים חרש לאמר״. What is חרש? That they made themselves pottery dealers and would cry out, 'Whoever wants to buy pots, come and buy!' — and all this so that no person would notice that they were spies. חרש לאמר — read it as חרס (pottery). And who were they? Phinehas and Caleb. ״וילכו ויבאו בית אשה זונה ושמה רחב וישכבו שמה״ — the king of Jericho detected them. What did Rahab do? She sought to hide them. Phinehas said to her: 'I am a priest, and a priest is likened to an angel, as it says (Malachi 2:7): ״כי שפתי כהן ישמרו דעת ותורה יבקשו מפיהו כי מלאך ה׳ צבאות הוא״; and likewise a prophet is likened to an angel, for concerning Moses it is written (Numbers 20:16): ״וישלח מלאך ויוציאנו ממצרים״, and it is written (Judges 2:1): ״ויעל מלאך ה׳ מן הגלגל אל הבוכים״, and that is Phinehas.' Therefore he said to her: 'When I wish, I am seen, and when I do not wish, I am not seen.' And this is what is written (Joshua 2): ״ותקח האשה את שני האנשים ותצפנו״ — and it does not say ותצפנם, to teach you that she hid only Caleb. But the spies whom Moses sent were fools, as it says (Proverbs 10:18): ״ומוציא דבה הוא כסיל״ — end quote. And what the Midrash says, that they were fools, is because they corrupted themselves afterward — for Moses chose righteous men, and at the command of HaShem; therefore he called them by the term אנשים, in the language of (I Samuel 1:11): ״זרע אנשים״. But they corrupted their mission, and concerning them Moses said (Deuteronomy 32:20): ״כי דור תהפוכות המה בנים לא אמון בם״ — this teaches that they were examined and found righteous, and then turned back to wickedness. And this is ״ומוציא דבה הוא כסיל״ — once they brought out an evil report, they became fools."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:32:28.915447709Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}